**6 HORNBLOWERS SIGN** Purpose Assess Teres Minor function Procedure Shoulder elevated to 90 in the scapular plane with elbow flexed to 90 Ask the patient to maintain external rotation Positive Sign Unable to maintain the position or external rotation Indicates Teres minor tear or severe rotator cuff pathology\n</SYSTEM_MESSAGE>","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":17,"source":"SYSTEM","type":"SYSTEM_MESSAGE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-14T09:57:17Z","content":"The following is a <SYSTEM_MESSAGE> not actually sent by the user. It is provided by the system as important information to pay attention to.\n\n<SYSTEM_MESSAGE>\n[Message] timestamp=2026-09-14T09:57:06Z sender=a7e55962-d1c0-4e42-9dcc-d29979c73d47 priority=MESSAGE_PRIORITY_HIGH content=---**I TESTS FOR LATERAL EPICONDYLITIS TENNIS ELBOW** ---**1 COZENS TEST** Purpose To assess lateral epicondylitis Tennis Elbow Procedure • Patient makes a fist • Elbow flexed to 90 • Forearm pronated • Wrist extended and radially deviated against resistance Positive Sign Pain over the lateral epicondyle Indicates Lateral epicondylitis ---**2 MILLS TEST** Purpose To assess lateral epicondylitis Procedure • Patient sits with the elbow extended • Forearm pronated • Therapist passively flexes the wrist while maintaining elbow extension Positive Sign Pain over the lateral epicondyle Indicates Lateral epicondylitis ---**3 MAUDSLEYS TEST MIDDLE FINGER TEST** Purpose To assess lateral epicondylitis especially involvement of the Extensor Digitorum Communis EDC Procedure • Elbow extended • Forearm pronated • Patient resists extension of the middle finger Positive Sign Pain over the lateral epicondyle Indicates Lateral epicondylitis ---**II TEST FOR MEDIAL EPICONDYLITIS GOLFERS ELBOW** ---**4 GOLFERS ELBOW TEST MEDIAL EPICONDYLITIS TEST** Purpose To assess medial epicondylitis Golfers Elbow Procedure • Patient sits with the elbow extended • Forearm supinated • Therapist passively extends the wrist and fingers while maintaining elbow extension Positive Sign Pain over the medial epicondyle Indicates Medial Epicondylitis Golfers Elbow ---**III NERVE COMPRESSION TEST** ---**5 TINELS SIGN CUBITAL TUNNEL** Purpose To assess ulnar nerve irritation or compression Procedure • Slightly flex the elbow • Tap gently over the ulnar nerve behind the medial epicondyle Positive Sign Tingling or electric shock sensation along the ring and little fingers Indicates Cubital Tunnel Syndrome Ulnar nerve entrapment ---**IV LIGAMENT STABILITY TESTS** ---**6 VALGUS STRESS TEST** Purpose To assess the Medial Ulnar Collateral Ligament MCL/UCL Procedure • Elbow slightly flexed 20 30 • Stabilize the humerus • Apply a valgus force to the forearm Positive Sign Pain or excessive medial joint opening Indicates Medial collateral ligament injury ---**7 VARUS STRESS TEST** Purpose To assess the Lateral Collateral Ligament LCL Procedure • Elbow slightly flexed 20 30 • Stabilize the humerus • Apply a varus force to the forearm Positive Sign Pain or excessive lateral joint opening Indicates Lateral collateral ligament injury\n</SYSTEM_MESSAGE>"}
{"step_index":18,"source":"SYSTEM","type":"SYSTEM_MESSAGE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-14T09:57:17Z","content":"The following is a <SYSTEM_MESSAGE> not actually sent by the user. It is provided by the system as important information to pay attention to.\n\n<SYSTEM_MESSAGE>\n[Message] timestamp=2026-09-14T09:57:12Z sender=b0b6fc47-18db-4c28-91e7-af9ea5ee9dcd priority=MESSAGE_PRIORITY_HIGH content=--- **WRIST SPECIAL TESTS** --- **I CARPAL TUNNEL SYNDROME TESTS** --- **1 PHALENS TEST** Purpose To assess Carpal Tunnel Syndrome Median nerve compression Procedure • Ask the patient to flex both wrists maximally • Dorsum of both hands are pressed together • Hold for 60 seconds Positive Sign Pain tingling or numbness in the thumb index middle and lateral half of ring finger Indicates Carpal Tunnel Syndrome --- **2 REVERSE PHALENS TEST PRAYER TEST** Purpose To assess Carpal Tunnel Syndrome Procedure • Ask the patient to press both palms together • Maintain maximum wrist extension for 60 seconds Positive Sign Pain or paresthesia in the median nerve distribution Indicates Carpal Tunnel Syndrome --- **3 TINELS SIGN WRIST** Purpose To assess Median nerve irritation Procedure • Tap gently over the median nerve at the carpal tunnel Positive Sign Tingling or electric shock sensation in the median nerve distribution Indicates Carpal Tunnel Syndrome --- **II TENDON PATHOLOGY TEST** --- **4 FINKELSTEINS TEST** Purpose To assess De Quervains Tenosynovitis Procedure • Patient places the thumb inside the fist • Fingers wrap around the thumb • Examiner passively deviates the wrist towards the ulnar side Positive Sign Pain over the radial styloid Indicates De Quervains Tenosynovitis --- **III LIGAMENT STABILITY TEST** --- **5 WATSON SCAPHOID SHIFT TEST** Purpose To assess Scapholunate ligament instability Procedure • Apply pressure over the scaphoid tubercle • Move the wrist from ulnar deviation to radial deviation Positive Sign Pain or palpable clunk Indicates Scapholunate ligament injury --- **IV THUMB TEST** --- **6 GRIND TEST** Purpose To assess 1st Carpometacarpal CMC joint osteoarthritis Procedure • Apply axial compression to the thumb • Simultaneously rotate the thumb metacarpal Positive Sign Pain or crepitus Indicates 1st CMC joint osteoarthritis --- **I TESTS FOR BONE LIGAMENT CAPSULE JOINT INSTABILITY** --- **1 ULNAR FOVEA TEST** Purpose To assess Triangular Fibrocartilage Complex TFCC injury Procedure • Press the examiners thumb into the ulnar fovea between the ulnar styloid and flexor carpi ulnaris tendon Positive Sign Pain or tenderness over the ulnar fovea Indicates TFCC injury or ulnotriquetral ligament injury --- **2 ULNO MENISCO TRIQUETRAL DORSAL GLIDE TEST** Purpose To assess ulnotriquetral ligament and TFCC pathology Procedure • Stabilize the distal ulna • Apply a dorsal glide to the triquetrum while assessing pain and movement Positive Sign Pain or excessive movement Indicates Ulnotriquetral ligament injury or TFCC lesion --- **3 BUNNELL LITTLER TEST** Purpose To differentiate intrinsic muscle tightness from joint capsule tightness Procedure • Flex the PIP joint with the MCP joint extended • Repeat with the MCP joint flexed Positive Sign • PIP flexion improves when MCP is flexed Intrinsic muscle tightness • PIP flexion remains limited Capsular tightness Indicates Intrinsic muscle contracture or capsular restriction\n</SYSTEM_MESSAGE>"}
{"step_index":19,"source":"SYSTEM","type":"SYSTEM_MESSAGE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-14T09:57:17Z","content":"The following is a <SYSTEM_MESSAGE> not actually sent by the user. It is provided by the system as important information to pay attention to.\n\n<SYSTEM_MESSAGE>\n[Message] timestamp=2026-09-14T09:57:13Z sender=5263ee06-8b32-49ca-9765-df839f9ee61c priority=MESSAGE_PRIORITY_HIGH content=---**SPECIAL TESTS KNEE JOINT** ---**I TESTS FOR LIGAMENT INTEGRITY** ---**A COLLATERAL LIGAMENTS** ---**1 VALGUS STRESS TEST** PURPOSE To assess the integrity of the medial collateral ligament MCL PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine • Knee is slightly flexed approximately 20 30 to unlock the joint • Stabilize the lateral aspect of the knee • Apply a valgus force to the lower leg while stabilizing the thigh POSITIVE SIGN • Excessive medial joint opening compared with the opposite side • Pain along the medial knee INDICATES MCL injury/insufficiency ---**2 VARUS STRESS TEST** PURPOSE To assess the integrity of the lateral collateral ligament LCL PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine • Knee is slightly flexed approximately 20 30 • Stabilize the medial aspect of the knee • Apply a varus force to the lower leg POSITIVE SIGN • Excessive lateral joint opening • Pain along the lateral knee INDICATES LCL injury/insufficiency ---**B CRUCIATE LIGAMENTS** ---**3 LACHMAN TEST** PURPOSE To assess the integrity of the anterior cruciate ligament ACL PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine • Knee flexed approximately 20 30 • Stabilize the femur with one hand • Hold the proximal tibia with the other hand • Pull the tibia anteriorly POSITIVE SIGN • Excessive anterior translation of tibia • Soft/absent firm end feel compared with the normal side INDICATES ACL injury/tear Very important Lachman is generally more sensitive for ACL injury than the anterior drawer test particularly in the acute knee ---**4 ANTERIOR DRAWER TEST** PURPOSE To assess the ACL PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine • Hip flexed and knee flexed to 90 • Stabilize the foot • Grasp the proximal tibia with both hands • Pull the tibia anteriorly POSITIVE SIGN • \n<truncated 1634 bytes>\n DYSFUNCTION** ---**8 FLUCTUATION TEST** PURPOSE To detect knee joint effusion particularly larger effusions PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine with the knee relaxed/extended • Examiner compresses the fluid from one side of the knee toward the opposite side • Observe/palpate for a fluid wave POSITIVE SIGN • A palpable/visible fluid wave or fluctuation INDICATES Knee joint effusion ---**9 PATELLAR TAP TEST** PURPOSE To detect knee joint effusion PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine with knee extended • Gently press/compress the suprapatellar region to move fluid beneath the patella • Tap the patella downward against the femur POSITIVE SIGN • Patella rebounds/floats and taps the femur producing a palpable tap INDICATES Significant knee joint effusion ---**10 CLARKES TEST / PATELLAR GRIND TEST** PURPOSE To assess patellofemoral dysfunction particularly chondromalacia/patellofemoral pain PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine with the knee extended • Examiner places the hand proximal to the patella • Apply gentle downward pressure to the patella • Ask the patient to contract the quadriceps POSITIVE SIGN • Pain behind/around the patella • Inability to maintain quadriceps contraction because of pain INDICATES Possible patellofemoral dysfunction/chondral pathology ---**IV OTHER MEASUREMENT** ---**11 Q ANGLE** PURPOSE To assess patellofemoral alignment and the lateral pull on the patella MEASUREMENT Q angle is formed by the intersection of • Line 1 ASIS centre of patella • Line 2 centre of patella tibial tubercle PROCEDURE • Patient positioned with the knee in the appropriate standardized position • Identify and mark • ASIS • Centre of patella • Tibial tubercle • Use a goniometer to measure the angle between the two lines POSITIVE/ABNORMAL FINDING An increased Q angle may indicate increased lateral patellar alignment/pull and may be associated with patellofemoral dysfunction INDICATES • Patellofemoral malalignment • Predisposition to lateral patellar tracking/instability\n</SYSTEM_MESSAGE>","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":20,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-14T09:57:17Z","content":"I've received the formatted tests for the Ankle/Foot, Shoulder, Elbow, Wrist, and Knee. \n\nJust waiting on the remaining 3 agents (Cervical, Hip, and Lumbosacral) to finalize their work.","thinking":"**Prioritizing Tool Usage**\n\nI'm currently focused on how best to approach the available tools. I'm prioritizing the most specific tools for each task. It's becoming clear that efficiency is key, so choosing the right tool upfront is essential for streamlined operations. My goal is to minimize wasted steps.\n\n\n"}
{"step_index":21,"source":"SYSTEM","type":"SYSTEM_MESSAGE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-14T09:57:51Z","content":"The following is a <SYSTEM_MESSAGE> not actually sent by the user. It is provided by the system as important information to pay attention to.\n\n<SYSTEM_MESSAGE>\n[Message] timestamp=2026-09-14T09:57:31Z sender=5e38b999-b288-4523-a3fd-900c6ee6a19e priority=MESSAGE_PRIORITY_HIGH content=**SPECIAL TESTS LUMBO SACRAL REGION** --- **I TEST FOR LUMBAR INSTABILITY** --- **1 PASSIVE LUMBAR EXTENSION TEST** PURPOSE To assess lumbar spine instability PROCEDURE • Patient lies prone • Examiner lifts both the patients legs together by holding around the ankles • The legs are elevated while the knees remain extended • Observe the patients symptoms and lumbar response POSITIVE SIGN • Development/reproduction of low back pain • Feeling of heaviness or instability in the lumbar region INDICATES Lumbar segmental instability --- **II TESTS FOR MUSCLE DYSFUNCTION** --- **2 90 90 STRAIGHT LEG RAISING TEST** PURPOSE To assess hamstring muscle tightness/flexibility PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine • Flex the hip and knee to approximately 90 • Stabilize the thigh • Slowly extend the knee while maintaining hip flexion POSITIVE SIGN • Inability to achieve full knee extension • Increased resistance/tightness in the posterior thigh INDICATES Hamstring tightness/shortness --- **3 TRENDELENBURG TEST** PURPOSE To assess hip abductor muscle function especially gluteus medius PROCEDURE • Patient stands on one leg • Ask the patient to lift the opposite foot from the floor • Observe the position of the pelvis POSITIVE SIGN • Pelvis drops on the side of the lifted/non weight bearing leg INDICATES Weakness of the hip abductors of the weight bearing leg particularly gluteus medius --- **III TESTS FOR NEUROLOGICAL DYSFUNCTION** --- **4 STRAIGHT LEG RAISING TEST SLR/LASÈGUES TEST** PURPOSE To assess lumbosacral nerve root irritation particularly due to PIVD PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine • Keep the knee fully extended • Slowly raise the patients straight leg by flexing the hip • Note the angle and symptoms produce\n<truncated 1018 bytes>\n bend forward maximally • Measure the distance between the marks again POSITIVE SIGN Insufficient increase in distance during forward flexion INDICATES Reduced lumbar flexion/mobility commonly seen in conditions such as ankylosing spondylitis --- **7 FABER / PATRICKS TEST** FABER Flexion ABduction External Rotation PURPOSE To assess hip and sacroiliac joint pathology PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine • Place the tested leg in • Hip flexion • Abduction • External rotation • Place the lateral ankle over the opposite thigh • Stabilize the opposite pelvis • Gently press the flexed knee downward POSITIVE SIGN • Pain reproduced in the hip/groin suggests hip pathology • Pain in the sacroiliac region may indicate SI joint pathology • Restricted ROM may also be significant INDICATES Hip or sacroiliac joint dysfunction --- **8 GAENSLENS TEST** PURPOSE To assess sacroiliac joint dysfunction/pathology PROCEDURE • Patient lies near the edge of the table • One hip and knee are maximally flexed toward the chest • The opposite leg is allowed to hang off the edge of the table • Apply gentle pressure to produce opposite movements at the pelvis POSITIVE SIGN • Reproduction of pain in the sacroiliac region INDICATES Sacroiliac joint dysfunction --- **9 GILLETS TEST** PURPOSE To assess sacroiliac joint mobility PROCEDURE • Patient stands • Examiner palpates the PSIS and sacrum • Patient flexes one hip and knee lifting the foot from the floor • Observe movement of the PSIS relative to the sacrum POSITIVE SIGN • Abnormal or restricted movement of the PSIS compared with the opposite side INDICATES Sacroiliac joint hypomobility/dysfunction --- **10 MARCHS TEST** PURPOSE To assess sacroiliac joint movement/dysfunction PROCEDURE • Patient stands • Examiner palpates the PSIS • Patient marches by lifting one knee at a time • Observe the movement of the PSIS POSITIVE SIGN • Abnormal/asymmetrical movement of the PSIS INDICATES Sacroiliac joint dysfunction or altered mobility\n</SYSTEM_MESSAGE>","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":22,"source":"SYSTEM","type":"SYSTEM_MESSAGE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-14T09:57:51Z","content":"The following is a <SYSTEM_MESSAGE> not actually sent by the user. It is provided by the system as important information to pay attention to.\n\n<SYSTEM_MESSAGE>\n[Message] timestamp=2026-09-14T09:57:32Z sender=8aa9bbc8-11eb-4ae4-afd7-3c10ae615a0e priority=MESSAGE_PRIORITY_HIGH content=**CERVICAL SPECIAL TESTS** --- **1 Spurlings Test /Foraminal Compression Test/** **PURPOSE** To assess cervical nerve root compression /cervical radiculopathy/ **PROCEDURE** • Patient sits comfortably • Extend laterally flex and rotate the head towards the affected side • Apply gentle downward compression on the head **POSITIVE SIGN** Radiating pain numbness or tingling into the upper limb **INDICATES** • Cervical nerve root compression • Cervical radiculopathy --- **2 Cervical Distraction Test** **PURPOSE** To assess cervical nerve root compression **PROCEDURE** • Patient lies supine • Therapist gently grasps the head and applies an upward traction force **POSITIVE SIGN** Relief of neck or arm pain **INDICATES** • Cervical radiculopathy • Foraminal compression --- **3 Upper Limb Tension Test /ULTT/** **PURPOSE** To assess neural tissue mobility and detect peripheral nerve or cervical nerve root involvement --- **ULTT 1 /MEDIAN NERVE BIAS/** **Procedure** • Depress the shoulder • Abduct the shoulder to 110 • Supinate the forearm • Extend the wrist and fingers • Externally rotate the shoulder • Slowly extend the elbow • Finally side flex the neck away if required **Positive Sign** • Pain tingling or numbness along the median nerve distribution • Difference in symptoms compared to the opposite side **Indicates** • Median nerve tension • Cervical radiculopathy --- **ULTT 2A /MEDIAN NERVE BIAS/** **Procedure** • Shoulder depression • Elbow extension • External rotation of shoulder • Forearm supination • Wrist and finger extension **Positive Sign** Reproduction of median nerve symptoms **Indicates** Median nerve irritation --- **ULTT 2B /RADIAL NERVE BIAS/** **Procedure** • Shoulder depre\n<truncated 1979 bytes>\nplaces tension on the cervical nerve structures **POSITIVE SIGN** • Reproduction of the patients radiating pain/paresthesia into the upper limb **INDICATES** Possible cervical nerve root or brachial plexus involvement --- **3 PINCH GRIP TEST** **PURPOSE** To assess the function of the anterior interosseous nerve /AIN/ and flexor pollicis longus/index FDP function **PROCEDURE** • Ask the patient to make a pinch/OK sign by touching the tip of the thumb to the tip of the index finger • Observe the shape of the circle formed **POSITIVE SIGN** • Patient cannot make a proper round OK circle • Instead the pinch becomes flattened/pulp to pulp **INDICATES** Anterior interosseous nerve palsy/dysfunction --- **4 CARPAL COMPRESSION TEST** **PURPOSE** To assess for carpal tunnel syndrome due to median nerve compression **PROCEDURE** • Patients wrist is in a neutral position • Examiner applies direct pressure over the carpal tunnel with the thumbs/fingers • Maintain pressure for approximately 30 seconds **POSITIVE SIGN** • Reproduction of • Pain • Tingling • Numbness • Paresthesia in the median nerve distribution **INDICATES** Carpal Tunnel Syndrome / median nerve compression --- **5 HAND ELEVATION TEST** **PURPOSE** To assess for carpal tunnel syndrome/median nerve compression **PROCEDURE** • Patient raises both hands above the head • Maintain the elevated position for approximately 1 2 minutes • Ask the patient about symptoms **POSITIVE SIGN** • Reproduction of pain numbness or tingling in the median nerve distribution **INDICATES** Possible Carpal Tunnel Syndrome --- **6 FROMENTS SIGN** **PURPOSE** To assess the ulnar nerve particularly the function of adductor pollicis **PROCEDURE** • Ask the patient to hold a piece of paper between the thumb and index finger • Examiner attempts to pull the paper away **POSITIVE SIGN** Patient compensates by • Flexing the IP joint of the thumb using flexor pollicis longus **INDICATES** Ulnar nerve palsy/weakness of adductor pollicis\n</SYSTEM_MESSAGE>","truncated_fields":["content"]}
