{"step_index":24,"source":"SYSTEM","type":"SYSTEM_MESSAGE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-14T09:58:44Z","content":"The following is a <SYSTEM_MESSAGE> not actually sent by the user. It is provided by the system as important information to pay attention to.\n\n<SYSTEM_MESSAGE>\n[Message] timestamp=2026-09-14T09:58:16Z sender=cf0b36a8-31c9-4c03-afc1-803c399d2d36 priority=MESSAGE_PRIORITY_HIGH content=--- **I TESTS FOR HIP PATHOLOGY** --- **1 HIP SCOUR TEST** PURPOSE To assess for intraarticular hip pathology PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine • Flex the hip and knee • Apply a gentle axial load through the femur • Move the hip through flexion/extension and abduction/adduction while maintaining compression POSITIVE SIGN • Pain • Catching • Clicking • Crepitus • Restricted/abnormal movement INDICATES Possible intraarticular hip pathology such as degenerative or labral pathology --- **2 LEG ROLL TEST** PURPOSE To assess for intraarticular hip pathology and restriction of passive hip rotation PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine with the leg relaxed • Examiner gently rolls the leg from side to side producing internal and external rotation of the hip • Compare with the opposite side POSITIVE SIGN • Pain • Markedly increased or decreased hip rotation • Excessive external rotation compared with the opposite side INDICATES Possible hip joint pathology --- **II TEST FOR LABRAL LESION** --- **4 ANTERIOR LABRAL TEAR TEST** PURPOSE To assess for an acetabular labral lesion particularly an anterior labral lesion PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine • Flex the hip and bring it through the provocative combination of hip movement used to stress the anterior labrum • Reproduce the patients familiar symptoms • Compare with the opposite side POSITIVE SIGN • Reproduction of anterior hip/groin pain • Clicking catching or givingway sensation may occur INDICATES Possible acetabular labral lesion --- **III PEDIATRIC HIP PATHOLOGY** --- **5 BARLOWS TEST** PURPOSE To determine whether an infants hip is unstable and can be dislocated PROCEDURE • Infant lies supine • Flex the hip and knee • Stabilize the pelvis • Gently ad\n<truncated 1890 bytes>\nleg POSITIVE SIGN • Opposite thigh rises from the table hip flexor tightness • Knee extends possible rectus femoris tightness • Hip abducts possible TFL/ITB tightness INDICATES Hip flexor muscle tightness/contracture --- **V TESTS FOR HIP DISPLACEMENT** --- **11 NELATONS LINE** PURPOSE To assess the position of the greater trochanter in relation to the pelvis PROCEDURE • Patient lies in the appropriate position with the hip examined • Draw an imaginary line from the ASIS to the ischial tuberosity • Assess the position of the greater trochanter relative to this line POSITIVE FINDING • Greater trochanter lies above Nelatons line INDICATES Possible superior displacement of the femur/hip dislocation --- **12 BRYANTS TRIANGLE** PURPOSE To detect proximal displacement of the greater trochanter and apparent shortening of the femur PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine • From the ASIS draw a perpendicular line to the bed • Draw a line from the greater trochanter to meet this perpendicular • Compare the triangles on both sides POSITIVE FINDING • Shortening of the base of Bryants triangle on the affected side INDICATES Possible hip dislocation or proximal displacement of the femur --- **VI SACROILIAC JOINT TESTS** --- **14 GAENSLENS TEST** PURPOSE To assess SI joint pathology PROCEDURE • Patient lies near the edge of the table • One hip is flexed toward the chest • The opposite leg hangs over the edge • Apply gentle pressure to produce opposite pelvic movements POSITIVE SIGN • Reproduction of pain around the SI joint INDICATES Sacroiliac joint dysfunction/pathology --- **VII OTHER MUSCLE DYSFUNCTION TESTS** --- **17 TRIPOD SIGN** PURPOSE To assess hamstring tightness PROCEDURE • Patient sits with the legs extended • Examiner observes the patients sitting posture • Patient attempts to sit upright POSITIVE SIGN • Patient leans backward and supports themselves with their hands • Knee flexion may occur because of hamstring tightness INDICATES Hamstring tightness/shortness\n</SYSTEM_MESSAGE>","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":25,"source":"SYSTEM","type":"SYSTEM_MESSAGE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-14T09:58:44Z","content":"The following is a <SYSTEM_MESSAGE> not actually sent by the user. It is provided by the system as important information to pay attention to.\n\n<SYSTEM_MESSAGE>\n[Message] timestamp=2026-09-14T09:58:34Z sender=system priority=MESSAGE_PRIORITY_HIGH content=Subagent 8aa9bbc8-11eb-4ae4-afd7-3c10ae615a0e has gone idle. This was the subagent's most recent response: **CERVICAL SPECIAL TESTS** --- **1 Spurlings Test /Foraminal Compression Test/** **PURPOSE** To assess cervical nerve root compression /cervical radiculopathy/ **PROCEDURE** • Patient sits comfortably • Extend laterally flex and rotate the head towards the affected side • Apply gentle downward compression on the head **POSITIVE SIGN** Radiating pain numbness or tingling into the upper limb **INDICATES** • Cervical nerve root compression • Cervical radiculopathy --- **2 Cervical Distraction Test** **PURPOSE** To assess cervical nerve root compression **PROCEDURE** • Patient lies supine • Therapist gently grasps the head and applies an upward traction force **POSITIVE SIGN** Relief of neck or arm pain **INDICATES** • Cervical radiculopathy • Foraminal compression --- **3 Upper Limb Tension Test /ULTT/** **PURPOSE** To assess neural tissue mobility and detect peripheral nerve or cervical nerve root involvement --- **ULTT 1 /MEDIAN NERVE BIAS/** **Procedure** • Depress the shoulder • Abduct the shoulder to 110 • Supinate the forearm • Extend the wrist and fingers • Externally rotate the shoulder • Slowly extend the elbow • Finally side flex the neck away if required **Positive Sign** • Pain tingling or numbness along the median nerve distribution • Difference in symptoms compared to the opposite side **Indicates** • Median nerve tension • Cervical radiculopathy --- **ULTT 2A /MEDIAN NERVE BIAS/** **Procedure** • Shoulder depression • Elbow extension • External rotation of shoulder • Forearm supination • Wrist and finger extension **Positive Sign** Reproduction of median nerve symptoms **Indicates** Median nerve ir\n<truncated 2056 bytes>\nplaces tension on the cervical nerve structures **POSITIVE SIGN** • Reproduction of the patients radiating pain/paresthesia into the upper limb **INDICATES** Possible cervical nerve root or brachial plexus involvement --- **3 PINCH GRIP TEST** **PURPOSE** To assess the function of the anterior interosseous nerve /AIN/ and flexor pollicis longus/index FDP function **PROCEDURE** • Ask the patient to make a pinch/OK sign by touching the tip of the thumb to the tip of the index finger • Observe the shape of the circle formed **POSITIVE SIGN** • Patient cannot make a proper round OK circle • Instead the pinch becomes flattened/pulp to pulp **INDICATES** Anterior interosseous nerve palsy/dysfunction --- **4 CARPAL COMPRESSION TEST** **PURPOSE** To assess for carpal tunnel syndrome due to median nerve compression **PROCEDURE** • Patients wrist is in a neutral position • Examiner applies direct pressure over the carpal tunnel with the thumbs/fingers • Maintain pressure for approximately 30 seconds **POSITIVE SIGN** • Reproduction of • Pain • Tingling • Numbness • Paresthesia in the median nerve distribution **INDICATES** Carpal Tunnel Syndrome / median nerve compression --- **5 HAND ELEVATION TEST** **PURPOSE** To assess for carpal tunnel syndrome/median nerve compression **PROCEDURE** • Patient raises both hands above the head • Maintain the elevated position for approximately 1 2 minutes • Ask the patient about symptoms **POSITIVE SIGN** • Reproduction of pain numbness or tingling in the median nerve distribution **INDICATES** Possible Carpal Tunnel Syndrome --- **6 FROMENTS SIGN** **PURPOSE** To assess the ulnar nerve particularly the function of adductor pollicis **PROCEDURE** • Ask the patient to hold a piece of paper between the thumb and index finger • Examiner attempts to pull the paper away **POSITIVE SIGN** Patient compensates by • Flexing the IP joint of the thumb using flexor pollicis longus **INDICATES** Ulnar nerve palsy/weakness of adductor pollicis\n</SYSTEM_MESSAGE>","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":26,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-14T09:58:44Z","content":"3 Special Test: Cervical Spine:\nTest for Vertigo & Dizziness tests: Vertebral Artery Test Dizziness Test etc.\nTest for Thoracic Outlet Syndrome: Adson's test, Roos stress Test etc.\nTest for Neurological symptoms: Foraminal Compression Test, Distraction Test, Shoulder Depression Test etc.\nPFDP 3 Cervical Special Tests .pdf\n**CERVICAL SPECIAL TESTS** --- **1 Spurlings Test /Foraminal Compression Test/** **PURPOSE** To assess cervical nerve root compression /cervical radiculopathy/ **PROCEDURE** • Patient sits comfortably • Extend laterally flex and rotate the head towards the affected side • Apply gentle downward compression on the head **POSITIVE SIGN** Radiating pain numbness or tingling into the upper limb **INDICATES** • Cervical nerve root compression • Cervical radiculopathy --- **2 Cervical Distraction Test** **PURPOSE** To assess cervical nerve root compression **PROCEDURE** • Patient lies supine • Therapist gently grasps the head and applies an upward traction force **POSITIVE SIGN** Relief of neck or arm pain **INDICATES** • Cervical radiculopathy • Foraminal compression --- **3 Upper Limb Tension Test /ULTT/** **PURPOSE** To assess neural tissue mobility and detect peripheral nerve or cervical nerve root involvement --- **ULTT 1 /MEDIAN NERVE BIAS/** **Procedure** • Depress the shoulder • Abduct the shoulder to 110 • Supinate the forearm • Extend the wrist and fingers • Externally rotate the shoulder • Slowly extend the elbow • Finally side flex the neck away if required **Positive Sign** • Pain tingling or numbness along the median nerve distribution • Difference in symptoms compared to the opposite side **Indicates** • Median nerve tension • Cervical radiculopathy --- **ULTT 2A /MEDIAN NERVE BIAS/** **Procedure** • Shoulder depression • Elbow extension • External rotation of shoulder • Forearm supination • Wrist and finger extension **Positive Sign** Reproduction of median nerve symptoms **Indicates** Median nerve irritation --- **ULTT 2B /RADIAL NERVE \n<truncated 33059 bytes>\nt • Predisposition to lateral patellar tracking/instability\n\n--------------------\n\n10 Special Test: Ankle & Foot Complex:\nTests for Ligament Stability: Anterior Drawer Test, Talar Tilt Test etc.\nTests for Vascular Conditions (e.g., DVT): Homan's Test, Moses Test etc.\nPFDP 10 ankle foot special tests.pdf\n---**I TESTS FOR LIGAMENT STABILITY** ---**1 ANTERIOR DRAWER TEST ANKLE** PURPOSE To assess the integrity of the anterior talofibular ligament ATFL and lateral ankle stability PROCEDURE • Patient sits or lies supine • Keep the ankle in a neutral or slight plantar flexed position • Stabilize the distal tibia with one hand • Grasp the heel/talus with the other hand • Draw the foot/talus anteriorly POSITIVE SIGN • Excessive anterior translation compared with the opposite ankle • Soft/absent end feel • Pain may be reproduced INDICATES ATFL injury/lateral ankle instability ---**2 TALAR TILT TEST** PURPOSE To assess the integrity of the lateral ankle ligaments particularly the calcaneofibular ligament CFL PROCEDURE • Patient sits or lies supine • Stabilize the distal leg • Grasp the calcaneus/heel • Tilt the calcaneus into inversion POSITIVE SIGN • Excessive inversion compared with the opposite side • Pain or a soft end feel INDICATES CFL injury/lateral ankle instability ---**II TESTS FOR VASCULAR CONDITIONS DVT** ---**3 HOMANS TEST** PURPOSE Traditionally used to assess for deep vein thrombosis DVT of the lower limb PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine • Knee is extended • Examiner supports the leg • Passively dorsiflex the ankle POSITIVE SIGN • Pain in the calf during passive ankle dorsiflexion INDICATES Traditionally considered suggestive of DVT ---**4 MOSES TEST** PURPOSE Traditionally used to assess for DVT PROCEDURE • Patient lies supine with the leg relaxed • Compress the calf anteroposteriorly • Then compress the calf laterally POSITIVE SIGN • Pain produced by anteroposterior compression but not by lateral compression INDICATES Traditionally considered suggestive of DVT","thinking":"**Prioritizing Tool Usage**\n\nI'm focusing on the most efficient tool utilization. It's becoming clearer that prioritizing specific tools will drastically improve my problem-solving ability. The goal is to always select the tool best suited for the immediate task at hand, regardless of how broad a command may be. This will keep work on the project organized.\n\n\n**Refining Tool Selection**\n\nI'm now honing my tool selection process based on two critical instructions. First, I recall the rules for avoiding unnecessary tool calls (cat, ls, grep, etc.). Second, I'm making a conscious effort to explicitly list all relevant tools before executing any of them. The format is being applied, I believe, as I get used to it.\n\n\n**Clarifying Agent Assignments**\n\nI'm now clarifying how agents should handle multiple PDFs. The instructions emphasize separate lines for each PDF's topics. I understand now: each PDF's topic list needs a newline separator from other PDFs, but within a PDF, the continuous topic format needs no extra newlines. Also, I've received an image with a specific list that will be used.\n\n\n**Listing Shoulder Tests**\n\nI'm cataloging shoulder tests, starting with impingement (Hawkins-Kennedy, Neer's), labral lesions (O'Brien, KIM), scapular dyskinesia, and muscle pathology. Moving to the elbow, I'm identifying tests for ligament instability and tennis/golfer's elbow. Finally, I'm noting neurological tests like Pinch Grip. The wrist and hand tests for carpal tunnel, bone instability, and circulation are also being listed.\n\n\n**Expanding Test Inventories**\n\nI'm now cataloging tests for the lumbo-sacral region, including lumbar instability, muscle dysfunction, neurological issues, and joint dysfunction. I'm also adding tests for hip joint pathology, labral lesions, and pediatric h