{"step_index":5,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-14T10:32:10Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nASS OF CARDIO ---1. Demographic Data Definition: The statistical characteristics of a patient.\r\n•\tClinical Significance:\r\n•\tAge: Certain conditions are age-specific (e.g., COPD and coronary artery disease are more common in older adults, whereas asthma or cystic fibrosis may present in childhood).\r\n•\tGender: Males have a statistically higher risk for early-onset ischemic heart disease.\r\n•\tOccupation: Helps identify exposure to occupational hazards.\r\n2. Chief Complaint \r\n•\tDefinition: The primary symptom or problem that prompted the patient to seek physiotherapy, recorded in the patient’s own words.\r\n•\tClinical Significance: Helps prioritize the treatment plan. Common cardiopulmonary chief complaints include dyspnea (shortness of breath), angina (chest pain), fatigue, claudication (leg pain on walking), and chronic cough.\r\n3. History of Present Illness (HPI) & Functional Limitation Definition: A detailed chronological account of the chief complaint from its onset to the present.\r\n•\tClinical Significance:\r\n•\tOnset and Duration: Is the dyspnea sudden (e.g., pulmonary embolism, pneumothorax) or gradual (e.g., COPD, congestive heart failure)?\r\n•\tAggravating and Relieving Factors: Orthopnea (breathlessness on lying flat) relieved by sitting up suggests congestive heart failure. Angina relieved by rest suggests stable coronary disease.\r\n•\tFunctional Limitation: Evaluating how the illness impacts Activities of Daily Living (ADLs). The physiotherapist must quantify this using scales (e.g., NYHA Functional Classification for heart failure or the MRC Dyspnea Scale). Example: \"Patient is unable to climb one flight of stairs without severe breathlessness.\"\r\n4. Past Medical and Surgical History \r\n•\tDefinition: A record of all previous illnesses, hospitalizations, and surgeries.\r\n•\tClinical Significance:\r\n•\tMedical: History of hypertension, diabetes mellitus, asthma, or previous myocardial infarction (MI) significantly alters the risk profile and exercise prescription.\r\n•\tSurgical: P\n<truncated 4490 bytes>\ne. Purulent (yellow/green) indicates infection; rust-colored indicates lobar pneumonia; frothy-pink indicates pulmonary edema.\r\n4. Palpation and Percussion Tactile and Vocal Fremitus: The vibration felt on the chest wall when the patient speaks (e.g., saying \"99\").\r\n•\tIncreased fremitus indicates consolidation (e.g., pneumonia).\r\n•\tDecreased fremitus indicates increased air or fluid in the pleural space (e.g., pneumothorax, pleural effusion).\r\n•\tThoracic Mobility: Palpating the ribs and thoracic spine for segmental mobility, stiffness, and pain.\r\n•\tPercussion: Tapping the chest wall to determine underlying tissue density.\r\n•\tResonant: Normal air-filled lungs.\r\n•\tHyper-resonant: Hyperinflation (COPD) or pneumothorax.\r\n•\tDull/Stony Dull: Solid organ, consolidation, or pleural effusion.\r\n5. Auscultation \r\nListening to the internal sounds of the body using a stethoscope.\r\n•\tBreath Sounds:\r\n•\tNormal: Vesicular (soft, low-pitched over peripheral lung fields), Bronchial (harsh, over trachea).\r\n•\tAdventitious (Abnormal): Crackles/Crepitations (fluid in alveoli/airways), Wheezes (bronchospasm/narrowing), Pleural friction rub (pleurisy).\r\n•\tHeart Sounds:\r\n•\tNormal: S1 (AV valve closure) and S2 (Semilunar valve closure).\r\n•\tAbnormal: S3 gallop (indicates heart failure/volume overload), murmurs (valvular stenosis or regurgitation).\r\n6. Measurements Chest Expansion: Measured using a standard inch-tape at three levels: Axillary, Nipple, and Xiphisternum. Normal excursion is 3-5 cm. Decreased or asymmetrical expansion indicates restrictive lung diseases, lobar collapse, or unilateral paralysis.\r\n•\tBMI and Waist-Hip Ratio:\r\n•\tBMI: Weight (kg) / Height (m²).\r\n•\tWaist-Hip Ratio: Evaluates central obesity. Both are critical anthropometric measurements used to assess the risk of metabolic syndrome and cardiovascular disease. High ratios correlate directly with ischemic heart disease.\r\n\r\n\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-14T16:02:10+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>","truncated_fields":["content"]}
