{"step_index":8,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-14T10:36:39Z","content":"**Functional Assessment for Cardiovascular and Pulmonary Dysfunction** **Definition of Functional Assessment** Functional assessment evaluates an individuals capacity to perform activities of daily living and exercise determining the impact of cardiovascular and pulmonary dysfunction on overall quality of life and physical capability It provides objective data for exercise prescription and monitors disease progression or recovery **Borg Scale for Rate of Perceived Exertion RPE** • **Definition** The Borg RPE scale is a subjective tool used to measure an individuals perceived effort and fatigue during physical activity • **Scale Range** It ranges from 6 to 20 where 6 represents no exertion at all and 20 represents maximal exertion • **Theoretical Basis** The scale is designed to correlate with the individuals heart rate multiplied by 10 eg a rating of 12 corresponds roughly to a heart rate of 120 beats per minute • **Clinical Significance** It is crucial for patients taking medications like beta blockers which alter heart rate response allowing them to self monitor exercise intensity safely **Modified Borg Scale** • **Definition** A revised version of the original Borg scale that uses a non linear scale from 0 to 10 • **Scale Ratings** 0 is rest or no breathlessness 3 is moderate breathlessness 5 is severe and 10 is maximal breathlessness • **Clinical Application** It is highly sensitive to changes in dyspnea and leg fatigue making it the preferred scale for patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease COPD and heart failure during rehabilitation **Grades of Dyspnea** Dyspnea or shortness of breath is a primary limiting factor in cardiopulmonary diseases **Modified Medical Research Council mMRC Dyspnea Scale** • **Definition** A simple grading system that assesses the degree of baseline breathlessness related to physical activity • **Grade 0** I only get breathless with strenuous exercise • **Grade 1** I get short of breath when hurrying on the level or walking up a slight hill • **Gra\n<truncated 3409 bytes>\n* A laboratory based test using a treadmill with continuous ECG blood pressure and symptom monitoring to evaluate cardiac ischemia and maximal functional capacity **Theoretical Bases of Different Protocols for Maximal Exercise Testing** Maximal exercise protocols vary in their starting intensity and rate of progression to accommodate different fitness levels and clinical conditions **Bruce Protocol** • **Theoretical Basis** A maximal multi stage treadmill protocol characterized by large abrupt increases in workload It is designed to quickly bring healthy subjects or athletes to their maximal effort • **Procedure** Speed and incline increase every 3 minutes starting at 1 7 miles per hour mph and a 10 percent grade • **Clinical Application** Best suited for younger or physically active individuals being evaluated for coronary artery disease **Modified Bruce Protocol** • **Theoretical Basis** Designed for older adults or patients with known cardiac disease who cannot tolerate the initial high workload of the standard Bruce protocol • **Procedure** It adds two preliminary stages stage 1 starts at 1 7 mph with 0 percent grade and stage 2 is 1 7 mph with 5 percent grade • **Clinical Application** Prevents premature test termination due to musculoskeletal limitations or sudden cardiac stress allowing a safer and more gradual cardiovascular adaptation **Balke Protocol** • **Theoretical Basis** A constant speed protocol with gradual incremental increases in workload primarily driven by elevation changes rather than speed • **Procedure** The treadmill speed is kept constant usually 3 3 mph while the grade increases by 1 percent every minute • **Clinical Application** Highly effective for elderly patients deconditioned individuals and those with peripheral arterial disease PAD because the constant walking 