{"step_index":5,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-17T14:27:20Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nInvestigations for Musculoskeletal Disorders BPT 3rd Year Physiotherapy | Easy-language university exam notes | 10-mark answers Reference: David J. Magee, Orthopedic Physical Assessment (assessment principles). Simplified study notes; not verbatim textbook content. Contents • 1. Blood Investigations and Markers • 2. Ultrasonography (USG) • 3. X-ray (Radiography) • 4. Computed Tomography (CT Scan) • 5. Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) Page 1 BPT Physiotherapy Study Notes1. Blood Investigations and Markers Definition: Blood investigations are laboratory tests performed on a blood sample to detect inflammation, infection, autoimmune disease, muscle injury, and metabolic problems affecting bones and joints. In simple words: Blood tests help identify whether a musculoskeletal problem may be due to infection, inflammation, an immune disorder, or a metabolic problem. Aims • Detect infection and inflammation. • Identify autoimmune diseases such as rheumatoid arthritis. • Assess bone and mineral metabolism. • Detect muscle damage. • Support diagnosis and differential diagnosis. • Monitor disease activity and response to treatment. Important blood investigations • CBC (Complete Blood Count): Measures red cells, white cells, hemoglobin, and platelets. Increased WBCs may suggest infection; low hemoglobin may indicate anemia; platelets may rise during inflammation. • ESR (Erythrocyte Sedimentation Rate): Measures how far red blood cells settle in one hour. It may rise in inflammatory disorders and infections. It is a nonspecific marker and cannot identify the exact disease. • CRP (C-Reactive Protein): A protein produced mainly by the liver during inflammation. It rises in infection and inflammatory disorders and is useful for monitoring treatment response. It usually changes faster than ESR. • RF (Rheumatoid Factor): An autoantibody that may be present in rheumatoid arthritis. It supports diagnosis but can also be positive in other conditions or some healthy older adults. • Anti-\n<truncated 8112 bytes>\nthe MRI table. • Acquire images in different planes and sequences. • Use contrast material only when indicated. Indications • Ligaments: ACL, PCL, collateral ligament, and selected shoulder injuries. • Tendons and muscles: Rotator cuff or Achilles tears, muscle tears, hematomas, and tendinopathy. • Joints: Meniscal tears, cartilage injury, effusion, synovitis, and osteonecrosis. • Spine: Disc herniation, spinal stenosis, nerve-root compression, spinal cord abnormalities, and infection. • Tumors: Extent of bone marrow and surrounding soft-tissue involvement. Possible findings • Ligament or tendon tears. • Muscle injury and cartilage damage. • Bone marrow edema. • Disc herniation or nerve compression. • Soft-tissue masses. Advantages • No ionizing radiation. • Excellent soft-tissue contrast. • Shows bone marrow abnormalities. • Useful for ligaments, tendons, cartilage, and spinal structures. • Images can be obtained in multiple planes. Limitations • Expensive and often takes longer than X-ray or CT. • Claustrophobia may be a problem. • Some implants or metallic foreign bodies need special safety assessment. Page 8 BPT Physiotherapy Study Notes• Movement can reduce image quality. • Some abnormalities also occur in people without symptoms. Physiotherapy importance: Supports assessment of soft-tissue and spinal conditions, helps plan rehabilitation precautions, and may guide referral. MRI findings must be matched with symptoms and examination. Conclusion: MRI is valuable for soft-tissue, bone marrow, and spinal disorders and does not use ionizing radiation.\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-17T19:57:20+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\n<USER_SETTINGS_CHANGE>\nThe user changed setting `Model Selection` from Claude Opus 4.6 (Thinking) to Gemini 3.1 Pro (High). No need to comment on this change if the user doesn't ask about it. If reporting what model you are, please use a human readable name instead of the exact string.\n</USER_SETTINGS_CHANGE>","truncated_fields":["content"]}
